WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.237
Toki: Vera, what's inside a transformer?

00:00:02.638 --> 00:00:08.186
Vera: One block, stacked. GPT-2 small has 12; GPT-3 has 96.

00:00:08.586 --> 00:00:10.053
Toki: Like floors in a tall building.

00:00:11.653 --> 00:00:12.748
Toki: What flows up through them?

00:00:13.148 --> 00:00:16.163
Vera: Each token's vector, straight up: the residual stream.

00:00:16.563 --> 00:00:21.218
Vera: Every part reads it, computes, and adds its result back. Nothing overwrites.

00:00:21.618 --> 00:00:22.829
Toki: So it all just piles up.

00:00:24.429 --> 00:00:25.902
Toki: Where do tokens talk to each other?

00:00:26.302 --> 00:00:29.698
Vera: Only in attention. It moves information between positions.

00:00:30.098 --> 00:00:32.121
Vera: So “her” can pull in “Mia”.

00:00:32.521 --> 00:00:34.045
Toki: The only group chat in the building.

00:00:35.645 --> 00:00:36.543
Toki: And the MLP?

00:00:36.943 --> 00:00:41.458
Vera: Each token alone: expand about four times, a nonlinearity, project back.

00:00:41.858 --> 00:00:44.721
Vera: It holds roughly two thirds of a dense model's parameters.

00:00:45.121 --> 00:00:47.794
Vera: Research suggests much factual recall happens there.

00:00:49.395 --> 00:00:50.881
Toki: Why add? Why not replace?

00:00:51.281 --> 00:00:55.804
Vera: Without residual connections, a signal through a hundred layers is distorted beyond recovery.

00:00:56.204 --> 00:00:57.982
Vera: And gradients vanish on the way back.

00:00:58.382 --> 00:00:59.143
Toki: Who figured that out?

00:00:59.543 --> 00:01:02.154
Vera: Computer vision: ResNet, 2015.

00:01:02.554 --> 00:01:05.163
Vera: Norms keep each sublayer's input at a stable scale.

00:01:06.763 --> 00:01:07.810
Toki: How big is a real one?

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:12.383
Vera: The guide's calculator says Llama 3 8B has 8.03 billion.

00:01:12.783 --> 00:01:13.873
Toki: And the MLP's share?

00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:18.592
Vera: 70%. Real formulas, minus tiny norm and bias terms.

00:01:20.192 --> 00:01:21.465
Toki: Can I climb a block myself?

00:01:21.865 --> 00:01:24.829
Vera: Step through it and try the calculator, in The block chapter.

00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:27.907
Toki: One floor at a time. Adding, never erasing.
